<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/8th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berber Revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/739">739</a>–<a href="/years/743">743 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Berber revolts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> Revolt was a series of uprisings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> between 739 and 743 CE against the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>. It was triggered by grievances among Berber converts, including Arab officials' refusal to treat them as equals in the division of conquered land. The revolt showed that the empire's peripheral populations could not be held by force alone and contributed to the Umayyad decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berber-revolt.jpg" alt="Berber Revolt" width="320" height="187" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Omar-toons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morocco_and_the_Maghreb_after_the_Berber_Revolt.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> had done much of the fighting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-conquest-of-hispania">Umayyad conquest of Hispania</a>, yet they received a smaller share of the spoils and the harsher duties. The governor Yazid ibn Abi Muslim branded the hands of his Berber guard and was assassinated in 721, and under Ubayd Allah ibn al-Habhab Muslim Berbers were still enslaved, with girls sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. Kharijite preachers of Sufrite and later Ibadite persuasion, arriving from the 720s, promised an order in which all Muslims would be equal regardless of ethnicity. When Omar ibn al-Moradi, deputy governor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>, declared the Berbers a conquered people and seized their property, the tribes of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> rose.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Led by Maysara al-Matghari, the Ghomara, Berghwata and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miknasa">Miknasa</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> while the Ifriqiyan army was away on an expedition against Byzantine Sicily, killed al-Moradi, and swept Umayyad garrisons from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sous">Sous</a>. Caliph Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik sent Kulthum ibn Iyad al-Qushayri with 30,000 men raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, but the army was destroyed at the Battle of Bagdoura in 741, and about 10,000 survivors under Kulthum's nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balj-ibn-bishr">Balj ibn Bishr</a> were besieged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a>.</p><p>The revolt spread east and north. Hanzala ibn Safwan, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, saved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> leaders Uqasha ibn Ayub al-Fezari and Abd al-Wahid ibn Yazid al-Hawwari. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> the Berber garrisons north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> mutinied in October 741 and marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a>. The Andalusian governor Abd al-Malik ibn Qatn al-Fihri let Balj's Syrians cross the strait, and in 742 they crushed all three Berber columns, after which Balj deposed Abd al-Malik and had him tortured to death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> but never recovered the rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, where independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> states arose, among them the Barghawata state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamesna">Tamesna</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> and the Midrarid emirate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, Alfonso I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> seized the abandoned northwestern forts and emptied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> valley into a frontier zone. The loss of Syrian troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> weakened the army on which Marwanid rule rested, and historians count the revolt among the causes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-revolution">Abbasid Revolution</a> less than a decade later.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Berber Revolt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The great <b>Berber Revolt</b> in North Africa broke out in 739–740 over discriminatory taxation, demands for slaves and unequal treatment of Berber Muslims who had fought in the conquests.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
